    "[Proof Comment 1]: You should use pseudo-radial projection instead of radial projection since **Warning** radial projection is not PL. \\BF{Pseudo-radial projection.} Let \\(F\\) be a cell in \\(A\\) and let\n\\(\\partial F=\\bar F\\setminus F\\) be the boundary of \\(F\\).  Pick\n\\(z\\in F\\) and consider the radial projectin \\(p: \\bar F\\setminus\n\\{z\\}\\to \\partial F\\), \\(p(y)=\\) ``the intersection of the ray from\n\\(z\\) to \\(y\\) and \\(\\partial F\\)''.  The map \\(p\\) is not PL in\ngeneral (see [Rourke-Sanderson, p.6]) but we can construct a PL\nversion as follows.\nChoose an open neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(z\\) in \\(F\\) such that \\(\\bar\nU\\) is a convex polyhedron.  Choose a simplicial subdivision \\(K\\) of \\(\\bar\nF\\setminus U\\).  For every simplex \\(C\\) in \\(K\\) with vertices\n\\(v_0,\\ldots,v_k\\), define \\(q_C\\) to be the unique affine map from\n\\(|C|\\) to the convex hull of \\(p(v_0),\\ldots,p(v_k)\\) such that\n\\(q_C(v_i)=p(v_i)\\) for \\(i=0,\\ldots,k\\).  It is easy to see that the\nmaps \\(q_C\\) glue together and give a PL map \\(q: \\bar F\\setminus U\\to\n\\partial F\\), which we will refer to as {\\it a pseudo-radial\n  projection}.  A similar construction of the same name is used in\n[Rourke-Sanderson, p.21].\n\n\\medbreak\n\n\n\nLet \\(D\\) be another convex polyhedron complex such that \\(|D|\\) is compact.\n\n\\begin{lemma*} Let \\(f:D\\to A\\) be a PL map and let \\(F\\) be a cell of\n  \\(A\\) such that \\(\\dim F > \\dim D\\).  Then there exists a PL map \\(g:D\\to A\\)\n  such that \\(g(D)\\subset (f(D)\\setminus F)\\cup\\partial F\\) and\n  \\(g(x)=f(x)\\) for all \\(x\\in D\\) satisfying \\(f(x)\\notin F\\).\n\\end{lemma*}\n\n\\proof The set \\(f(D)\\) has dimension \\(\\leq \\dim D\\) since \\(f\\) is\nPL.  Therefore, \\(F\\setminus f(D)\\) is non-empty.\nPick \\(z\\in F\\) and form a pseudo-radial projection \\(q: \\bar F\\setminus U\\to \\partial F\\) as\nabove, where \\(U\\) is chosen so that \\(\\bar U\\) is disjoint from\n\\(f(D)\\).  Extend \\(q\\) to a map \\(A\\setminus U\\to A\\setminus F\\) by\nsetting \\(q(y)=y\\) for \\(y\\notin F\\).  Clearly, the extended map \\(q\\)\nis PL,  \\(g:=q\\circ f\\) is also PL, and \\(g\\) has the required properties.\\qed",
    "[Proof Comment 1]: You should use pseudo-radial projection instead of radial projection since **Warning** radial projection is not PL. \\BF{Pseudo-radial projection.} Let \\(F\\) be a cell in \\(A\\) and let\n\\(\\partial F=\\bar F\\setminus F\\) be the boundary of \\(F\\).  Pick\n\\(z\\in F\\) and consider the radial projectin \\(p: \\bar F\\setminus\n\\{z\\}\\to \\partial F\\), \\(p(y)=\\) ``the intersection of the ray from\n\\(z\\) to \\(y\\) and \\(\\partial F\\)''.  The map \\(p\\) is not PL in\ngeneral (see [Rourke-Sanderson, p.6]) but we can construct a PL\nversion as follows.\nChoose an open neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(z\\) in \\(F\\) such that \\(\\bar\nU\\) is a convex polyhedron.  Choose a simplicial subdivision \\(K\\) of \\(\\bar\nF\\setminus U\\).  For every simplex \\(C\\) in \\(K\\) with vertices\n\\(v_0,\\ldots,v_k\\), define \\(q_C\\) to be the unique affine map from\n\\(|C|\\) to the convex hull of \\(p(v_0),\\ldots,p(v_k)\\) such that\n\\(q_C(v_i)=p(v_i)\\) for \\(i=0,\\ldots,k\\).  It is easy to see that the\nmaps \\(q_C\\) glue together and give a PL map \\(q: \\bar F\\setminus U\\to\n\\partial F\\), which we will refer to as {\\it a pseudo-radial\n  projection}.  A similar construction of the same name is used in\n[Rourke-Sanderson, p.21].\n\n\\medbreak\n\n\n\nLet \\(D\\) be another convex polyhedron complex such that \\(|D|\\) is compact.\n\n\\begin{lemma*} Let \\(f:D\\to A\\) be a PL map and let \\(F\\) be a cell of\n  \\(A\\) such that \\(\\dim F > \\dim D\\).  Then there exists a PL map \\(g:D\\to A\\)\n  such that \\(g(D)\\subset (f(D)\\setminus F)\\cup\\partial F\\) and\n  \\(g(x)=f(x)\\) for all \\(x\\in D\\) satisfying \\(f(x)\\notin F\\).\n\\end{lemma*}\n\n\\proof The set \\(f(D)\\) has dimension \\(\\leq \\dim D\\) since \\(f\\) is\nPL.  Therefore, \\(F\\setminus f(D)\\) is non-empty.\nPick \\(z\\in F\\) and form a pseudo-radial projection \\(q: \\bar F\\setminus U\\to \\partial F\\) as\nabove, where \\(U\\) is chosen so that \\(\\bar U\\) is disjoint from\n\\(f(D)\\).  Extend \\(q\\) to a map \\(A\\setminus U\\to A\\setminus F\\) by\nsetting \\(q(y)=y\\) for \\(y\\notin F\\).  Clearly, the extended map \\(q\\)\nis PL,  \\(g:=q\\circ f\\) is also PL, and \\(g\\) has the required properties.\\qed"
